{
  "dom_id": "step-q20537",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/562596b4267c6a13ef13c4f20e7861b00675e85febc43653efd141d720900804.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Given that $f(0)=0,$ which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1\\,$ and $\\,x=0.$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{not extr.}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical lines $x=-1,$ and $x=0.$\n\n- Since we are told that $f(0)=0,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-1,0)$, the point at $x=0$ must be lower than the point at $x=-1.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/504a1af2c6d97200a3b211e8a7c67298082cd3cffbf866851afa63f7162dcce3.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and on $(-1,0)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and down to the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-1,0)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(0,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/6e6c1b9806b3dbf340255884722429a67dc3a7cd55d8cc0a0bbeeb48dcae1c87.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/10185719d14812cd4f58a592f106fe4fd81540ab6f6177f61d77bd6ec4e5d6c4.png)",
